<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hannah Arendt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Johanna Arendt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hannah Arendt was a German and American philosopher and political theorist, one of the most influential of the twentieth century. Born to a Jewish family in Linden, she studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a> and took her doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1929. Briefly imprisoned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> in 1933, she fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, escaped detention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1941. The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) established her reputation, followed by The Human Condition (1958) and by On Revolution and Eichmann in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> (1963), whose phrase the banality of evil stirred controversy. She died in 1975, leaving The Life of the Mind unfinished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt.jpg" alt="Hannah Arendt" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barbara Niggl Radloff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannah_Arendt_auf_dem_1._Kulturkritikerkongress,_Barbara_Niggl_Radloff,_FM-2019-1-5-9-16_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hannah Arendt was born Johanna Arendt in 1906 in Linden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, into a secular, educated and well-established Jewish family of merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. Her father died when she was seven. Her mother, an ardent Social Democrat, raised her on strict Goethean lines, with the reading of Goethe's complete works. After secondary school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> she studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-marburg">University of Marburg</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a>. She obtained her doctorate in philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1929 under Karl Jaspers, with a dissertation on love in Augustine. In 1929 she also married Günther Stern, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> briefly imprisoned Arendt for illegal research into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">antisemitism</a>. She fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with her mother, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> by night into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> she committed herself to the Jewish cause. She settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where she worked for Youth Aliyah, helping young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> emigrate to the British Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. When Germany invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> she was detained as an alien by the French government. She escaped and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 22 May 1941. There she became a writer and editor, worked for Jewish Cultural Reconstruction and became an American citizen in 1950. She taught at many American universities but declined tenure-track appointments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Origins of Totalitarianism of 1951, her first major book, examined the roots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> in three essays: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">Antisemitism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a> and Totalitarianism. The Human Condition of 1958 distinguishes political from social concepts, and labour from work, and develops a theory of political action in a public realm. Of human actions she singles out forgiving past wrong and promising future benefit. Between Past and Future collects eight essays written between 1954 and 1968, and her biography of Rahel Varnhagen, finished in 1938, was published in 1957.</p><p>Arendt never developed a systematic political theory and kept apart from schools of thought. The tradition most closely identified with her is civic republicanism, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> to Tocqueville, centred on active citizenship and collective deliberation. She is also remembered for the controversy over the trial of Adolf Eichmann and for the phrase the banality of evil. Her husband Heinrich Blücher died on 31 October 1970. She had a near-fatal heart attack while lecturing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in May 1974, and died of another in her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> apartment on 4 December 1975. Her ashes were buried beside Blücher's at Bard College, and her last work, The Life of the Mind, was left unfinished.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hannah Arendt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Liberal thinkers drew lessons from these regimes: Karl Popper’s The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945) defended democracy as the system that lets citizens remove their rulers without bloodshed, and <b>Hannah Arendt</b>’s The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) described how ideology and terror destroyed every independent space in society.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 <b>Hannah Arendt</b>’s The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951) and Carl Friedrich and Zbigniew Brzezinski’s Totalitarian Dictatorship and Autocracy (1956) made a single mass party, usually led by one man, a defining feature of totalitarian rule, alongside an official ideology, terror, and control of the economy, the media…</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The eternalist view sees antisemitism as separate from other prejudice, a transhistorical force culminating in the Holocaust; <b>Hannah Arendt</b> criticised it for answering “Why the Jews of all people?” with “Eternal hostility”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/antisemitism">Antisemitism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph Schumpeter and <b>Hannah Arendt</b> defined imperialism as expansion for the sake of expansion.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hannah Arendt</b> held that Nazism appealed as a totalitarian ideology because it offered clear solutions after the First World War and the Depression.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nazism">Nazism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hannah Arendt</b> traced the path from social Darwinist ethics to racist ideology; scholars dispute whether Haeckel's Monist League led to the Nazis, since the Monists were freethinkers whose organisations the Nazis banned in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Upper West Side, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.79,-73.98&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Upper West Side on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2738 707 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Hannah Arendt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3188 977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3188 977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.79,-73.98&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
